Project Vision and User Stories

Product Vision
For busy individuals, students, and budget-conscious consumers who struggle to decide what to eat based on their mood, cravings, or financial constraints, 
Bytez is a personalized food recommendation app that suggests meals tailored to emotional state, taste preferences, and budget patterns. Unlike generic 
food apps that only filter by cuisine or location, our product uses mood input and spending behavior to deliver smarter, more personalized, and financially 
aware meal suggestions.

User Stories
Damien: 
1.	As a student on a budget, I want to filter restaurants by price so that I can find affordable places to eat.
2.	As a person planning a special occasion, I want restaurant suggestions based on the event so that I can choose a place that fits the mood.
3.	As a tourist or visitor, I want to see restaurants near attractions so that I can eat somewhere convenient while exploring.

Emilio:
1.	As a user, I want to select or input my current mood so that I can receive food suggestions that match how I feel.
2.	As someone who is financially conscious, I want to set a budget or track my spending habits so that I can receive food recommendations that fit 
    within my financial limits. 
3.	As a person with the same taste, I want to receive personalized meal suggestions based on my past choices and cravings so that I can make quicker 
    and more satisfying decisions about what to eat.

Rafael:
1.	As a partygoer, I want to recommend places with a fun atmosphere so that I could bring more awareness.
2.	As a recommender, I want to be able to review restaurants and give them various ratings based on price, cleanliness, etc so that I know the full 
    vibe of the place.
3.	As a reviewer, I would want to filter the restaurants with my top rated reviews so that I don’t go to a place that I know I would dislike.
 
Brian G.:
1.	As a person who interacts with many apps, I want a clean interface that is user-friendly and the buttons make sense that showcases the function so 
    that it does what I want it to do.
2.	As a person who interacts with many barebones apps, I want an app interface that is colorful and looks fun and non-industrial so that it's not boring 
    and a pain to look at.
3.	As a person who likes to find hidden restaurants, I want a way where I can list off undiscovered restaurants that might be too small or speakeasies 
    that are often not listed on Google so that I can remember their location and how much I liked it.

Jay:
1.	As a Cal Poly student planning a first date, I want to filter restaurants by occasion (date night, casual, game day, family dinner) so that I can narrow 
    down to spots that match the vibe without scrolling through every restaurant in town.
2.	As a SLO local who eats out a lot, I want to filter by cuisine and minimum star rating at the same time so that I can find a highly-rated spot in a 
    specific cuisine without scrolling through low-rated ones.
3.	As a visitor new to SLO, I want to click on a restaurant and see its details (address, hours, price range, menu samples, photos) so that I can decide if 
    it's worth going without having to look it up on Google.
